@@s067 With <code>synchronized</code> even (1,1) disappears: the two bodies run one after the other, and whoever goes first reads the initial 0 of the other. Not because the lock prevents reordering inside the thread, but because mutual exclusion prevents mixing the bodies.
@@s068 <code>volatile</code>
@@s069 <code>volatile</code> is a keyword for fields: <code>volatile boolean running</code>. It is a <b>weak</b> form of synchronization (slide 65): it solves the visibility and reordering problems on that variable, without blocking anyone. Precisely because it does not block, it cannot prevent two threads from mixing inside a compound operation.
@@s070 A <code>volatile</code> variable is not kept hidden in the registers or caches of a CPU: the writes are made visible to the other CPUs.
@@s071 Accesses to volatile variables are <b>not reordered</b>.
@@s072 Reads and writes are <b>not blocking</b>.
@@s073 It does <b>not</b> guarantee mutual exclusion: <code>volatile int count; count++</code> remains a race condition.
@@s074 Visibility
@@s075 No reordering
@@s076 Mutual exclusion
